      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Objective",
          "content": "This experiment was conducted to determine the differences in the apparent ileal (AID) and total tract digestibility (ATTD) of nutrient and indispensable amino acid (IAA) in high-fiber diets with wheat middlings, rice bran or alfalfa meal fed to Duroc x Landrace x Yorkshire (DLY) and Duroc x Berkshire x Jiaxing (DBJ) growing pigs."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Methods",
          "content": "Eighteen DLY and 18 DBJ pigs were randomly allotted to a 2 x 3 factorial arrangement involving 2 pig breeds and 3 high-fiber diets. The experiment lasted 15 d with 10 d for diets adaptation, 3 d for feces collection and 2 d for digesta collection. Three diets were based on corn and soybean meal with 25% wheat middlings, rice bran and alfalfa meal respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Results",
          "content": "DBJ pigs had a greater (p<0.05) AID of Ile, Leu, Lys, Phe and Val and a lower (p<0.05) AID of Met than DLY pigs. The hindgut disappearance of ADF for DBJ pigs was greater (p<0.05) than DLY pigs. The ATTD of GE, DM, OM, NDF and ADF in wheat middlings diet were greater (p<0.05) than in rice bran and alfalfa meal diets. The hindgut disappearence of NDF and ADF in wheat middlings diet or rice bran diet were the highest or lowest (p<0.05), and those of alfalfa meal diet were the middle. Pigs fed rice bran diet had a greater (p<0.05) hindgut disappearance of GE, DM and OM and lower hindgut disappearance of NDF and ADF than pigs fed alfalfa meal diet."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "Conclusion",
          "content": "DBJ pigs had a high capacity for digestibility of fiber in the hindgut and most indispensable amino acids in the small intestine compared with DLY pigs. The digestibility of chemical contituents and indispensable amino acids was affected by the diets with different fiber sources."
        }
      ]},
